                <pb facs="0517" n="399" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg399"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00Sonora/780: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Guaymas</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bursley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guaymas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-04-22T13:00:00-06:00">April 22, 1929—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 23—1:45 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Practically entire stock fuel oil of railroad is at Guaymas and Empalme. With
                    operations of railway stopped, rebels have taken over the property and are using
                    it and all necessary supplies to further revolution movement. Strong oral
                    protest made to <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> authorities again today
                    for the protection of American interests by Commander and Consulate. If this
                    action and the taking of receipts by the company will protect Southern Pacific
                    interests with the Mexican Government recommend no further action be taken …
                    Please repeat to Embassy at Mexico City.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bursley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
